  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nAs an upper level low that has been cut off east of the region \nestablishes itself to the southwest of San Diego, with a high \npositioned to the north over northwestern Nevada in a rex blocking \npattern, the pressure gradient between the two are going to allow \nfor offshore winds to strengthen, initally out of the north to \nnortheasterly direction today, and then becoming more easterly \ntomorrow. These winds are expected to peak tomorrow, with gusts at \naround 35 to 55 mph for the inland valleys, and stronger gusts along \nthe slopes, with local gusts approaching 70 mph within some of the \nhigher terrain passes and canyons. As the low taps into some of the \nremnant tropical moisture from Simon, it will provide a slight \nincrease in humidity tomorrow, and there will be slight chance of a \nshower or two developing over the mountains during the afternoon \nhours. RH values will diminish to 15-25 percent by later this \nafternoon, but then gradually increase to 20 to 30 percent \nminimum values from Friday, with the influx of moisture. (see fire\nweather section below). Temperatures will be warmest for the \ncoastal locations, due to the offshore flow. This will also help \nto impede the marine layer from forming overnight. Highs will be \nthe warmest tomorrow, and for the coastal regions, ranging from \nupper 80s to low 90s, hence the reason for the Heat Advisory.\n\nThis weekend through next Wednesday...\n\nSanta Ana conditions will persist through Saturday, and then these \noffshore winds will begin to weaken. Temperatures will gradually \ncool throughout the weekend, and onshore flow will eventually \nreturn going into Sunday, whcih may bring about the onset of some \ndense fog for portions of the coastal areas during the early\nmorning hours. There will also be an approaching major shortwave \ntrough towards the middle part of next week, which will bring \nchances of precipitation back into the picture by late Tuesday \ninto Wednesday, along with the return of much cooler temperatures.",
